      <link>https://uwyn.net/@glaforge/116108354876248816</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 21 Feb 2026 11:21:35 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Comparing &lt;a href="https://uwyn.net/tags/Lean" class="mention hashtag" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"&gt;#&lt;span&gt;Lean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://uwyn.net/tags/Rocq" class="mention hashtag" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"&gt;#&lt;span&gt;Rocq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (formerly known as Coq)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I thought both were theorem provers, but it seems Rocq is more for building verifiable software, while Lean is for more classical math.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;So Lean might be a better fit for the math explorations &lt;a href="https://uwyn.net/tags/LLMs" class="mention hashtag" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"&gt;#&lt;span&gt;LLMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://uwyn.net/tags/AI" class="mention hashtag" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"&gt;#&lt;span&gt;AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="https://uwyn.net/tags/Agents" class="mention hashtag" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"&gt;#&lt;span&gt;Agents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; are doing for solving problems.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
